{"reference":"Jonah 2","book":"Jonah","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The prayer from the depths","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jonah’s prayer is shaped like a thanksgiving psalm, even though it describes overwhelming danger. Its images—Sheol, engulfing waters, mountain roots, and the pit—are poetic language for mortal peril and do not by themselves establish that Jonah died inside the fish. Readers differ over whether the prayer is wholly sincere, ironic in light of Jonah’s later conduct, self-focused, or a mixture of these possibilities; its placement between Jonah’s descent and release remains central to how the received narrative works.","verse_refs":["Jonah 2:1-10","Jonah 2:1-9","Jonah 2:2-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jonah_002_f67e0c9e35b62f6a965698d3"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"The great fish","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The text calls the creature a “great fish” and says that Jehovah commands it to bring Jonah onto dry land. It does not identify a species, explain how Jonah survived inside it, or state that Jonah died there. Specific identifications such as a whale or shark go beyond what the chapter says.","verse_refs":["Jonah 2:10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jonah_002_517a13c7e033b12956809667"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"A numbering difference","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Chapter and verse numbering differs between common English Christian editions and the Masoretic or Jewish tradition. What many English editions label Jonah 1:17—the fish’s appointment—begins chapter 2 in the Masoretic numbering, which ends this unit at its 2:11. Identifying the numbering system helps prevent confusion when comparing references to the fish and Jonah’s prayer.","verse_refs":["Jonah 2:10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jonah_002_593b34fd66108617d517ee5b"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The book beyond this chapter","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Jonah is preserved in the Masoretic text, the Old Greek Ionas, the Dead Sea Scrolls manuscripts 4Q76 and 4Q82, and other versions. The Greek translation is comparatively close to the Masoretic text, though translation and textual differences remain. Within the Masoretic Book of the Twelve, Jonah stands fifth, between Obadiah and Micah; proposed links with neighboring prophetic books supplement rather than replace Jonah’s own literary profile.","verse_refs":["Jonah 1:1-17","Jonah 3:1-10","Jonah 4:1-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jonah_002_4b62caaa61f998e39631b235"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}